  "worked_solution": "Using cofactor expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{11}+0\\cdot {C}_{12}+3\\cdot {C}_{13}+0\\cdot {C}_{14} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the $3\\times 3$ determinant by applying a Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =0\\cdot {C}_{21}+1\\cdot {C}_{22}+0\\cdot {C}_{23} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|=3\\cdot 5=15.\n$$",
